Z||000001||rogergarrison||02-05-2001||04:44 PM||||sometimes called 'draglink' too, it basicly connects both tie rods togeather on cars without rack and pinnion. Pretty ez if you have a fork to pop the ball joints. Youll have to get front alighnment after.||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||02-05-2001||05:44 PM||||so ball joint removal.seperation is necessary for this?<P>i am going to look at in in a minute.  the passport is a '97 that a golf pro owned, and has 83,000 miles, so i was expecting some work, but nothing this expensive!  it also needs new front bearings, and they ain't cheap!<P>thanks, Roger!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||02-06-2001||07:13 AM||||Not the balljoints on spindles, the link has its own smaller ball joints that connect to the spindles/tie rod ends. You dont have to remove springs, shocks or anything else. <P><BR>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bHooper:<BR><B>so ball joint removal.seperation is necessary for this?<P>i am going to look at in in a minute.  the passport is a '97 that a golf pro owned, and has 83,000 miles, so i was expecting some work, but nothing this expensive!  it also needs new front bearings, and they ain't cheap!<P>thanks, Roger!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 02-06-2001).]||||reg||1
